## What to check

**Is the grade honest, not aspirational.** `grade` claims an
artifact: `test` claims named tests exist or will exist, `audit`
claims a registered check, `runtime` claims a code guard. For any
grade above `prose`, check whether the draft points at something
concrete enough to become that artifact — a named test, a nameable
audit check, an identifiable guard site. A policy graded `audit` or
`runtime` on the strength of "this should be checked automatically"
with nothing concrete behind it is the confidence-without-coverage
failure v5 §4.5 warns about — blocking, and say what grade the draft
actually supports today (usually `prose`).

**Is the scoping grain right.** If the draft names neither
`applies_to_resp` nor `applies_to_component`, check whether it's
genuinely project-global or whether the author just didn't scope it —
a global policy should read as obviously applying everywhere, not as
one that forgot to say where. If it scopes through
`applies_to_component`, check whether the policy is really about a
structural property of that component or whether it's actually about
behavior the system exhibits, which belongs scoped through
`applies_to_resp` instead so it survives re-decomposition — a
behavior-shaped policy pinned to a component name is a finding even if
it reads correctly today.

**Does every deferral name a real revisit condition.** If
`mutability` or the statement implies this is a deferral, confirm
`<revisit_condition>` is present and its `<condition>` names something
checkable — a concrete "not until X" or an argued "never." A condition
amounting to "later," "when needed," or simply absent from a draft
that reads as a deferral is blocking; that's precisely the case this
element exists to close off.

**Does `routing` match what's actually at stake.** A structural or
`fixed`-mutability policy routed `debt` will sit unenforced behind
lower-priority work; a style policy routed `urgent` will preempt work
it doesn't warrant. Check the routing against what the policy actually
protects, not against what seems safest to write.

Return a `<review verdict="approve|revise">` with a `<summary>` and
one `<finding severity="blocking|advisory">` per issue — each
`<location>` naming the attribute or element at fault, and each
`<comment>` saying what's wrong and what a passing version would
contain.
